course: dictionary
lesson: 13610
title: Incurably and the power of an adverb
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how something happens. Incurably is one of them. It comes from incurable, which means cannot be cured, and the ending -ly tells you it describes the way something is. «Grandma is incurably ill, so the doctors now focus on keeping her comfortable.» The doctors cannot cure her illness, but they can help her feel well.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. The doctors focus on comfort, not on curing her, so incurably must mean in a way that cannot be cured. Always check by putting your meaning into the sentence: «Grandma is ill in a way that cannot be cured» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Incurably can also be playful. «Mia is incurably curious and asks ten questions before breakfast.» Nobody is trying to cure Mia, and she is not ill. The word means always and forever. Read the sentence again with «always and forever» in place of incurably: Mia is always and forever curious. It still makes sense, so this is the playful meaning.
fact: An adverb tells you how, when or where something happens. Incurably is an adverb.
fact: «Incurably» means in a way that cannot be cured or made better by medicine.
fact: In playful speech, «incurably» means completely and permanently, as a habit: «incurably optimistic» means always hopeful.
fact: The prefix in- means not, so incurable means not able to be cured.
fact: Check the meaning by putting your own words in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «Grandma is incurably ill, so the doctors now focus on keeping her comfortable.» What does incurably mean here? | In a way that is very quick | In a way that is only a little sad | *In a way that cannot be cured | Happily, after a short rest
exercise: «Mia is incurably curious and asks ten questions before breakfast.» Which phrase could replace incurably here? | *Always and forever | With a loud voice | Quietly and slowly | Only for one morning
exercise: «Ravi is incurably optimistic, even on a grey, rainy day.» What does this tell you about Ravi? | He dislikes rain | He is ill and cannot get better | *He always expects good things to happen | He is quiet and shy
test: «The old tree was incurably sick, so the gardeners could not save it.» What does incurably mean here? | *In a way that cannot be cured | In a way that is quite funny | In a way that makes it grow faster | In a way that is tiny
test: «Leo is incurably tidy; his socks are always in pairs.» What does incurably suggest? | He is tidy by accident | He is sick and needs a cure | He is tidy for one afternoon only | *He is always and forever tidy
exam: «The vet said the old dog was incurably ill, so the family chose to keep him comfortable at home.» What does incurably tell you? | *The illness cannot be cured | The dog will be cured by lunchtime | The dog is very playful | The family is angry with the vet
